You are validating a design for the top-level (non-fixup) portion of `rb_delete` in a red-black tree C library. Do NOT explore the repo broadly — I'm giving you the complete relevant context below; your job is to stress-test the design for correctness and memory safety, and refine the test plan. This is CS370 HW1 at /s/parsons/g/under/C836925517/cs370/HW1 (you may read src/rbtree.c, include/rbtree.h, tests/test_rbtree.c there if useful, but the design below is already complete and matches that code).

## Existing structure (already implemented, M1, working)
```c
typedef enum { RB_RED, RB_BLACK } rb_color_t;

typedef struct rb_node {
    char           *key;    /* owned copy */
    void           *value;  /* owned iff value_free != NULL */
    rb_color_t      color;
    struct rb_node *parent; /* NULL at root */
    struct rb_node *left;
    struct rb_node *right;
} rb_node_t;

struct rbtree {
    rb_node_t        *root; /* NULL = empty tree */
    size_t             size;
    rb_value_free_fn   value_free;
};
```
NIL is represented by plain NULL everywhere (no shared sentinel node) — `node_color(NULL) == RB_BLACK`. There's already `rotate_left`, `rotate_right`, `rb_malloc`/`rb_free` wrappers, and an `insert_fixup` that follows this same NULL-as-NIL convention with explicit parent-pointer walks. `rb_delete` is currently a stub: `(void)t; (void)key; return -1;`.

Header contract for rb_delete (include/rbtree.h, frozen, cannot change): "Removes key; frees the key copy and the value. Returns 0, or -1 if absent." No allocation-failure return code exists for delete — it must never allocate.

## Scope decision already made with the user
This slice implements ONLY the BST-splice orchestration of rb_delete (locate node, transplant/relink, splice out, free the physically-removed node, compute what fixup would need). `delete_fixup` is a **literal no-op stub** this slice — no rotation/recolor logic at all, not even the trivial "recolor x black" fallthrough. Full fixup-case implementation is explicitly deferred to a future slice/diff. Do not suggest implementing any fixup logic now — that's out of scope; just validate that the stub is a safe no-op for every input this slice can produce.

