input.content:
# M2 delete — Slice 1: top-level `rb_delete` (BST splice only)

## Context
M1 (insert/find/foreach/validate) is done and green (`make test` passes). `rb_delete` is
currently a stub (`return -1` always) and the fuzzer is a placeholder — both are M2's job per
CLAUDE.md. CLAUDE.md requires, for any algorithmic change like this (especially delete
fixup): a plan enumerating every fixup case and where each path frees memory, approved before
any code is written, and implementation in small single-case/single-file slices.

This plan covers **only the first slice**: `rb_delete`'s BST-splice orchestration (locate the
node, relink around it, free it, compute what a rebalance would need). `delete_fixup` is
implemented as a **literal no-op stub** this slice — no rotation/recolor logic at all. Real
fixup-case logic is out of scope here and will be its own follow-up slice(s)/plan. This scoping
was confirmed with the user directly (see AskUserQuestion in this conversation).

The splice design below was independently validated by a Plan subagent against the actual
`src/rbtree.c` — it walked every branch for use-after-free/leak/dangling-pointer/size
bookkeeping, checked the single-node-tree edge case, checked `x_parent` isn't stale across the
two-children/deeper-successor sub-branch, and hand-traced two concrete insertion sequences
against `insert_fixup` to find real, testable fixup-free cases. No bugs were found; one scoping
correction came back (below).

## Approach

Add three functions to `src/rbtree.c`, right where `rb_delete` currently lives:

**`subtree_min`** — leftmost node of a subtree (successor lookup for the two-children case):
```c
static rb_node_t *subtree_min(rb_node_t *n) {
    while (n->left != NULL) n = n->left;
    return n;
}
```

**`transplant`** — CLRS-style relink of subtree `v` into `u`'s position; guards `v == NULL`
since this codebase has no sentinel node to carry a parent pointer:
```c
static void transplant(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *u, rb_node_t *v) {
    if (u->parent == NULL) {
        t->root = v;
    } else if (u == u->parent->left) {
        u->parent->left = v;
    } else {
        u->parent->right = v;
    }
    if (v != NULL) {
        v->parent = u->parent;
    }
}
```

**`delete_fixup`** — no-op stub this slice. `x` (the node that moves into the spliced-out
position) may be `NULL`, and since NIL has no node object here, `x_parent` is threaded through
explicitly rather than read off `x->parent`:
```c
static void delete_fixup(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *x, rb_node_t *x_parent) {
    (void)t; (void)x; (void)x_parent;
    /* TODO(M2 slice 2+): rebalance. */
}
```

**`rb_delete`** replaces the stub body:
```c
int rb_delete(rbtree_t *t, const char *key) {
    rb_node_t *z = t->root;
    while (z != NULL) {
        int cmp = strcmp(key, z->key);
        if (cmp == 0) break;
        z = (cmp < 0) ? z->left : z->right;
    }
    if (z == NULL) {
        return -1;
    }

    rb_node_t *y = z;
    rb_color_t y_original_color = y->color;
    rb_node_t *x;
    rb_node_t *x_parent;

    if (z->left == NULL) {
        x = z->right;
        x_parent = z->parent;
        transplant(t, z, z->right);
    } else if (z->right == NULL) {
        x = z->left;
        x_parent = z->parent;
        transplant(t, z, z->left);
    } else {
        y = subtree_min(z->right);
        y_original_color = y->color;
        x = y->right;
        if (y->parent == z) {
            x_parent = y;
        } else {
            x_parent = y->parent;
            transplant(t, y, y->right);
            y->right = z->right;
            y->right->parent = y;
        }
        transplant(t, z, y);
        y->left = z->left;
        y->left->parent = y;
        y->color = z->color;
    }

    rb_free(z->key);
    if (t->value_free != NULL) {
        t->value_free(z->value);
    }
    rb_free(z);
    t->size--;

    if (y_original_color == RB_BLACK) {
        delete_fixup(t, x, x_parent);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Ownership/freeing (the CLAUDE.md-required "where each path frees memory" answer):** exactly
one node is ever freed per call — `z`, the node located by the initial search, whose key is the
one the caller asked to delete. This is CLRS's pointer-relinking delete, not a value-copy
variant: in the two-children case the successor `y` is spliced into `z`'s structural position
and takes `z`'s color, but `y`'s own `key`/`value` are never read or freed — only its position
moves. `z->key` and (if `value_free != NULL`) `z->value` are freed only after all pointer
surgery is complete, so there's no path where a freed node is still reachable. No allocation
occurs anywhere in `rb_delete`, matching the frozen header contract (no alloc-failure return
code exists for delete). No goto-cleanup is needed — confirmed against CLAUDE.md's
goto-cleanup rule, which applies to multi-allocation functions; this function never allocates
and every branch has a single unconditional exit.

## Fixup case enumeration (required by CLAUDE.md; design reference only — NOT implemented this slice)
For the future fixup slice(s), `delete_fixup(t, x, x_parent)` will need the standard CLRS
double-black cases, mirrored left/right like `insert_fixup` already does for insertion. Let `w`
= sibling of `x` (found via `x_parent`, since `x` may be `NULL`):
1. `w` red → recolor `w` black / `x_parent` red, rotate `x_parent` toward `x`'s side, re-derive
   `w`, continue loop.
2. `w` black, both `w`'s children black → recolor `w` red, move `x` up to `x_parent`, continue
   loop (or stop if new `x` is red or root).
3. `w` black, near nephew red, far nephew black → recolor near nephew black / `w` red, rotate
   `w` away from `x`'s side, re-derive `w`, fall through to case 4.
4. `w` black, far nephew red → recolor `w` to `x_parent`'s color, `x_parent` black, far nephew
   black, rotate `x_parent` toward `x`'s side, set `x` = root to terminate.
