   - Asked how many pushes had historically been made to `origin/main`, and whether pushing now would be the first GitHub-side evidence of work.
   - Explicitly decided: start pushing after every commit going forward, and asked the assistant to update `CLAUDE.md` to encode this policy, "stressing desire for proof of multiple-day work."
   - Asked to commit and push "the deletion changes as well" (which turned out to already be committed/pushed from a prior turn — only the CLAUDE.md policy update remained pending).
   - Most recent request: provide a command to upload a local Windows file (`c:/Users/andre/Downloads/CS370-HW1.pdf`) to this remote machine, into a file located next to the `HW1` directory, titled `HW1_Instructions`.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Red-black tree deletion (CLRS-style): BST node location, `transplant` (subtree relinking), in-order successor lookup via `subtree_min`, tracking `y_original_color`/`x`/`x_parent` to determine whether rebalancing (`delete_fixup`) is needed.
   - NIL representation choice: plain `NULL` (this codebase's existing convention, used throughout M1 code) vs. a shared sentinel node (`T.nil`) with a real `parent` field — evaluated and rejected in favor of keeping NULL-as-NIL and threading an explicit `x_parent` parameter into `delete_fixup`.
   - Ownership/memory-freeing contract: exactly one node (`z`, the located node) is ever freed per `rb_delete` call; the in-order successor `y` (when applicable) is relinked into `z`'s structural position without its own key/value being touched — this is CLRS's pointer-relinking delete, not a value-copy variant.
   - CLAUDE.md's mandated workflow: plan-mode approval before algorithmic changes; small slices (one case/file at a time); adversarial review before committing a milestone; commit message format `M<n>: <what>`; ≥8 meaningful commits; now also immediate `git push origin main` after every commit.
   - Toolchain gap: default `gcc` 11.5.0 lacks `-std=c23` support; must `module load gcc/15.2.0` every session (session-scoped, does not persist) before any `make` command — this is a pre-existing memorialized fact, not new this session.
   - Three required "green" gates before any change counts as done: `make test`, `make asan` (ASan/UBSan), `make memcheck` (valgrind `--leak-check=full`) — discovered that running `make asan` and `make memcheck` in parallel causes a build-artifact race (ASan-instrumented binaries left in `build/` satisfy `make memcheck`'s `all` prerequisite without rebuilding plain binaries, causing valgrind to fail against an ASan-linked binary); fixed by running them sequentially with a `make clean` in between.
   - Git provenance / proof-of-work distinction: local commit author/committer dates are self-reported and trivially backdated (`GIT_AUTHOR_DATE`, `git commit --date`, rebase) and prove nothing to a third party on their own; GitHub's server-side push-receipt timestamp (visible via contribution graph / Events API) is comparatively hard to fake and is the actual evidence of incremental work over time. `git reflog show refs/remotes/origin/main` was used to reconstruct this local clone's push history (two prior "update by push" entries with real dates) since the `gh` CLI was not installed on this machine (`gh: command not found`).
   - Plan-mode workflow tooling: `AskUserQuestion` for scoping decisions, `Agent` with `subagent_type: "Plan"` for design validation, `ExitPlanMode` to request approval, writing the plan to a dedicated plan file at `/s/parsons/g/under/C836925517/.claude/plans/groovy-napping-nebula.md`.
